turnkey-postgresql-19.0 (3) turnkey; urgency=low

  * The PostgreSQL server is a component instead of a piece of the shared
    tree. The recipe no longer includes mk/turnkey/pgsql.mk and no longer
    names the server packages; it composes keel-linux/unit-postgresql as
    unit.d/postgresql, which fab resolves and applies and bt-layer records in
    the layer manifest as "units postgresql@<version>". Decision 0013 is the
    reason: a database is a component, so LAMP and LAPP can share one
    apache-php layer instead of being children of two different databases.

  * listen_addresses = '::1,127.0.0.1' is set by the component now, not by
    conf.d/main. It is the same line for the same reason, in the place that
    owns it: LAPP will carry the component without carrying this recipe, and a
    fix living here would have to be copied there. conf.d/main keeps the check
    that the built image has it, so the component is verified rather than
    trusted, and the boot test still proves what the cluster binds.

  * No other change to what the image gets. The layer built this way was
    compared to a layer built from the shared tree the way decision 0010
    compares them, with a second build of the shared tree to measure the noise
    the comparison is read against.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 11:30:00 +0000

turnkey-postgresql-19.0 (2) turnkey; urgency=low

  * Listen on both loopback addresses, named literally:
    listen_addresses = '::1,127.0.0.1'. Debian's default, 'localhost',
    binds the IPv4 loopback alone, because Debian's /etc/hosts maps ::1 to
    ip6-localhost and never to localhost, so an appliance built on this
    layer and reaching its database over IPv6 found nothing listening.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 07:30:00 +0000

turnkey-postgresql-19.0 (1) turnkey; urgency=low

  * Initial release of the PostgreSQL database layer for Keel, compatible
    with TurnKey Linux appliances. Built as a layer on core:
    bt-layer postgresql --parent core.

  * PostgreSQL 17 from Debian 13 (Trixie), UTF-8 cluster, listening on the
    loopback of both families, scram-sha-256 over TCP.

  * webmin-postgresql, so the database is administered from the Webmin
    panel core already carries, at https://[address]:12321.

  * No password in the published layer. common's conf/pgsql gives the
    postgres role the literal password 'postgres' when the build names
    none; conf.d/main removes it, so the role cannot authenticate until
    the first boot sets it from the instance description.

  * The password comes from secrets.db_password of the instance
    description, which renders to DB_PASS, which common's
    firstboot.d/35pgsqlpass already reads. firstboot.d/36pgsqlverify then
    connects as a client and says whether it arrived.

  * Adminer, lighttpd and php-fpm, which the upstream postgresql appliance
    bundles, are deliberately left out: they belong to the web stack that
    LAPP builds on this layer. So is accepting password authentication
    from anywhere, which that appliance's conf.d/main enables.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sat, 27 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
